# Manage services and access

An operator must configure the trusted identity providers and exact bootstrap
human identity before administration is available. Signing in alone gives a
new user read-only access.

## Create a service

1. Sign in using the catalogue's configured browser provider.
2. As a global administrator, create a service with a public alias, such as
   `widget`.
3. Open the permissions dashboard and grant the producer's trusted workload
   identity the `publish` role for that service.
4. Publish one known build, then inspect the service anonymously to confirm the
   metadata you intended to make public.

Aliases use 1–64 lowercase letters, digits, underscores or hyphens, starting with
a letter or digit. Keep private registry names and internal project details out
of the public alias.

## Choose the permission

| Role | Identity and scope | What it permits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `admin` | Human identity, global `*` scope | Manage services, releases and grants |
| `publish` | Workload identity, one existing service | Add and update that service's releases |

A workload publisher cannot delete releases, create services or grant access.
The workload subject is the immutable repository claim selected by operator
configuration: for Forgejo this is `repository_id`, rather than the display name
or repository URL. Supply the exact trusted issuer and repository ID.

The service accepts any valid build identity for an authorised repository,
including accepted pull-request builds. The producer chooses when to publish;
there is no additional main-branch or release-event condition.

## Revoke access or remove data

Administrators can remove a database grant from the permissions dashboard.
Revocation applies to the next request, including requests from an existing
browser session. The configured bootstrap administrator remains an administrator
until the operator changes configuration.

Deleting a release removes its live catalogue entry. Deleting a service also
removes its releases and service grants. These actions leave registry images
untouched. Restoring an older database backup restores the entries and grants
that existed in that backup.
